  "question": {
    "text": "A small block of mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>M</mtext></math> moves on a frictionless surface of an inclined plane, as shown in the figure. The angle of the incline suddenly changes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>. The block is initially at rest at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>. Assume that collisions between the block and the incline are totally inelastic (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>). Figure:<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0104_e0e314e0b5d5.png\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 157px;\" />​<br />\r<br />\rThe speed of the block at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, immediately before it leaves the second incline is -"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msqrt><msup><mtext>&#160;m&#160;s</mtext><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>",
